{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get started I wanted to let you know,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2280,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "today’s episode is brought to \nyou by "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4550,
    "dDurationMs": 6492,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space:  The Longest Goodbye. Available now on the PBS App and YouTube. So check out the link below. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11042,
    "dDurationMs": 5518,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We are the middle children of history. Born \ntoo late to explore Earth, and born too early  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to explore the universe —- to partially quote \nsomeone on the internet whose wisdom is only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matched by their anonymity. In the far future \nwe may have advanced propulsion technologies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27520,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like matter-antimatter engines and compact \nfusion drives that allow humans to travel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to other stars on timescales shorter than their \nown lives. But what if those technologies never  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38040,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "materialize? Are we imprisoned by the vastness \nof space—doomed to remain in the solar system  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44640,
    "dDurationMs": 8440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our origin? Perhaps not. A possible path to a \ncontemporary cosmic dream may just be to build a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53080,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ship which can support human life for several \ngenerations; a so-called generation ship."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65840,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Faster than light travel is almost certainly \nimpossible—so says Einstein’s special theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71000,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of relativity—and we rarely win when we bet \nagainst Einstein. That sounds like bad news  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77120,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the galactic future of humanity given that \nthe Milky Way is 100,000 lightyears across,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82920,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and there are relatively few stars within what \nmost would consider to be a reasonable commute.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89040,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that doesn’t mean we can’t reach for the \nstars. If we can build spacecraft capable of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94120,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reaching 80, 90, even 99% the speed of light \nthen relativistic time dilation would slow the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clock of the spacecraft relative to Earth’s. \nAt these speeds a single crew could reach an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interstellar destination up to 100s of light \nyears away within their own lifetimes. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112760,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such speeds would require some pretty out-there \npropulsion methods like matter-antimatter engines,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compact fusion reactors, or even black \nhole drives. And even if we eventually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124720,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do build such devices, there are a whole \nrange of dangers that uniquely arise when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130760,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traveling through the cosmos at such high \nspeeds, as we’ve discussed previously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, what if it turns out we have to travel the \nslow road? What if it proves impossible to send  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142720,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "humans any faster than a tiny fraction of the \nspeed of light? Or what if we decide we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149080,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really really need to leave Earth ASAP using \ntechnology that we at least understand today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155600,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, Here’s the scenario: Something is coming. It \ncould be a comet impact or catastrophic climate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162760,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse or the Tri-Solarian fleet. Whatever \nit is, there’s enough of an existential threat  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we decide to insure the future of our \nspecies by trying to settle another world.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174720,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quite naturally, NASA tasks PBS Space \nTime with planning a mission to settle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180200,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Proxima Centauri B in the Alpha-Centauri system. \nThis is the closest known exoplanet to Earth at a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185840,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mere 4.2 light years away. To keep things simple, \nlet’s pretend that we discovered that Proxima-B  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is already habitable so all we need to do \nis get some people there in good condition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197520,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We only have a few decades to make this happen, so \nultra-advanced propulsion is out of the question.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204040,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We launch whatever we can throw together in around \n30 years. The fastest ship we could conceivably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211000,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hope to build might reach speeds of 10% that of \nlight. That’s a 42 year journey—launch a crew in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218760,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their 20s and they’ll arrive at retirement age. \nMore likely our craft will travel much slower,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226200,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so that no crew that starts the journey will live \nto see its end. Assuming that cryogenics won’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233160,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be 100% reliable within decades—which is pretty \nfair—it sounds like we need to plan for a mission  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which multiple generations of humans are \nborn, live, and die en route, and that landfall  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is made by descendents of the launch crew. It \nsounds like we need to plan a generation ship."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are lots of decisions to make in how \nwe do this, but remember our constraint:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258080,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it has to be something we can plausibly \nlaunch in 30 years. We’re going to need  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to choose a propulsion system, a crew size \nand composition, life support systems, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267760,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "finally we need to ensure the mental, social and \ncultural wellbeing and stability of this group."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273560,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Starting with the propulsion method; this \ndetermines the speed we can travel, the potential  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278240,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size of the ship, and so the size and number of \ngenerations of the population we need to sustain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283960,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fastest vehicle ever built by humans is \nthe Parker Solar Probe, which accelerates by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289760,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blasting a propellant—hydrazine in this \ncase—using electrical power. Although it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294920,
    "dDurationMs": 6853,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was really more the gravitational assists that \nenabled the Parker to reach 700,000 kilometers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301773,
    "dDurationMs": 6747,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "per hour. If we could scale up this tech to \nsomething large enough to carry lots of humans then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308520,
    "dDurationMs": 9680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at this speed we could get our crew to Proxima-B \nin … 6,300 years. That’s like 200 generations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318200,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and roughly the length of recorded human \nhistory. It’s difficult to imagine that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323120,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nothing would go wrong in that much time. But we’re \nalso pretty sure we can get a ship to this speed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330120,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we should see if this timescale \nis at least feasible. Also, this is the speed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336240,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assumption made by French scientists Frédéric \nMarin and Camille Beluffi in a series of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "studies, and we’ll be coming back to their \nconclusions regarding a trip of this length."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346920,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll also consider a much faster \ncraft—one propelled by nuclear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350600,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fusion—smashing light elements together to \nform heavier elements plus lots of energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just like the Sun does. We haven’t yet managed \nto build a commercially viable fusion reactor,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360640,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let alone the sort of compact reactor we’d \nneed for a spacecraft. But there IS a fusion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366120,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technology that we’ve thoroughly mastered—and \nthat’s the thermonuclear explosion. There are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371920,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "various concepts for spacecraft that \naccelerate under a series of fusion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376600,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pulses—aka explosions—rather than sustained \nfusion reactions. These vary in sophistication  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383720,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the more advanced internal confinement \nengine of Project Daedalus to more achievable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if scarier proposals where you literally detonate \nthermonuclear explosions behind the craft,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395680,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like in Project Orion or the Enzmann starship, \nor into a forward sail like in the Medusa design."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403560,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Top speeds for some of these have been estimated \nat 30% lightspeed, but that’s highly optimistic.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410560,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A little under 10% is more realistic for a \nmature version of this technology. For us,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415800,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with our limited timeline, we’re going to assume \nwe can get to 3% lightspeed. That’s around 50  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422160,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "times faster than our conventional drive, so \ngets us to Proxima-B in a mere 140 years—just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428600,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "four or five generations. So, today we’re going \nto plan towards these two travel times—140 years  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434920,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if fusion pans out and 6300 years if not. We’ll \nhave teams working on both, and you can think of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442320,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these as representing the extreme boundaries of \nwhat we can achieve in the little time we have."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447480,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The next decision will influence all of the \nchoices that follow. How many people are we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453040,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sending? This determines the size of the ship \nor ships and the resources we need to bring.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the most important factor determining \npopulation size on a generation ship is the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "issue of genetic diversity. There are two aspects \nto this: how many people are needed to ensure a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469080,
    "dDurationMs": 8600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "healthy multigenerational crew during the journey, \nand how many are needed to healthily populate a new planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477680,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A 6300 year journey means 200 generations \ngive or take. If the genetic diversity of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the starting population isn’t sufficient there \nwill be genetic health issues en route. Marin and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Beluffi explore this question in a 2018 paper. \nThey use Monte Carlo simulations to calculate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the minimum number of humans that would be needed \nto avoid many of the potential genetic pitfalls,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500440,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also accounting for various forms \nof misfortune such as a random  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disaster eliminating a third of the population, \ndifferent infertility rates, and even an overall  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509880,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“chaotic factor” intrinsic to any human \nexploration. From all of this they came  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514400,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up with the minimum numbers needed to \nachieve a sustainable population during  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the journey. They conclude that we need to \nlaunch with a crew of at least 100, who will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multiply to a population of 500—and that’s \nthe level to support for most of the journey."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529680,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How big a ship does it take to \ncomfortably carry 500? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "SpaceX’s Starship is supposed \nto be able to carry 100. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539560,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the equivalent of 5 of those at least? However \nthat doesn’t include the space needed for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545880,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "systems to support 500 lives long term. For that \nwe’re definitely going to need a bigger boat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553600,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Missions around the solar system don’t \nneed to be luxurious. But centuries or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559720,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "millenia long trips to Proxima-B will \nneed some home comforts. Like gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Living in zero gravity or microgravity \nhas clear negative effects on health,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the most well documented being on bone \ndensity. To avoid our travelers reaching  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Proxima-B as Wall-E-esque gelatinous \nblobs, we need artificial gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581720,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve discussed previously how this could be \ndone. There’s only one way, and fortunately  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s not that complicated. The ship’s \nhabitats need to be spun in a circle to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "give 1-g of centrifugal acceleration, \nperfectly mimicking Earth’s surface  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity. There are lots of designs \nfor centrifugal artificial gravity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602880,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the simplest might be a rotating ring habitat. \nA 100m radius ring would need to rotate 3 times  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610600,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "per minute to replicate Earth gravity. That \nseems not completely crazy, so let’s move on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617440,
    "dDurationMs": 8360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The next step is to feed our crew Another study \nled by the French team finds that we’d need 0.45  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625800,
    "dDurationMs": 8680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "km^2 for an omnivorous and balanced diet. Our \n5 Starships have a surface area of about 1%  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634480,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that. So we either send 500 starships \njust to feed our crew, or find a way to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641080,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produce food more efficiently. That 0.45 km^2 \nis dominated by the space for raising livestock,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649400,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so burger night is the first thing we’ll have \nto cut. It’s possible to get the required area  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655280,
    "dDurationMs": 9120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down 0.015 km^2 if we grow nutrition-dense crops \nlike sweet potatoes using our best hydroponic or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664400,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aeroponic systems. That’s just 30 starships worth \nof farm, so we’re back in the realm of the sane."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671840,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The crew is also going to need protein. \nNow maybe we can get the quantity and variety  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677080,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from an efficient veggie source, especially \nwith a little genetic tinkering. But if not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683760,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are plausible meat options. Now lab-grown meat \ntechnology is a bit speculative at the moment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but there’s a very well established carnivorous \noption suited to the less squeamish interstellar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695920,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traveler. I’m talking about insects. For example, \nmealworms can be farmed at high densities and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702520,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "provide extreme protein richness. One to a few \nStarships worth of mealworm might do the trick."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709840,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Overall, we’re going to need something \nlike 6 to 10 times our crew’s living  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space for food production. And that’s for \na pretty boring and slightly crawly diet.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720040,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But maybe there are some gourmet yam and \ngrub recipes just waiting to be discovered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725040,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A bigger challenge than food is the water, which \nour travelers need in order to grow that food,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731160,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and also in order to just live. An adult \nhuman needs around 2 liters of water per day,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736640,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "give or take. 500 humans need 1000 liters \nper day—that’s a cubic meter weighing a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742480,
    "dDurationMs": 7284,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metric ton. Our 140 year journey may \nbe able to haul the required 50,000 tons of water —just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749764,
    "dDurationMs": 6676,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "barely—but forget about it for our 6300 year \nslog. In either case we’re going to want very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756440,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good water recycling. Just recently, the ISS \nreached a new milestone of 98% water recycling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763920,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "efficiency. Now if that’s as good as we get for our \n“fast” mission we need a more reasonable 500 ton  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772440,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supply of reserve water—perhaps one Starship \nworth of water storage in terms of volume."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778880,
    "dDurationMs": 8760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For our 6-millenia-slog we need 50 times that. So \nour generation ship just doubled in size just to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787640,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "haul enough water. And remember that we haven’t \neven considered water used and lost growing food.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794640,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe add as much water again for 100 starships in \nwater. In order for the long trip to be plausible,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800760,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we may need to focus on improving our water \nrecycling—get it to at least 99.5% efficiency,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807920,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which brings the reserve storage requirement \ndown a factor of four to a similar scale as our farm requirement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815520,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is perhaps one upside to \nneeding to store all this water,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that’s that water can double as radiation \nshielding. About one meter depth of water  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surrounding habitats is enough to stop \nmost dangerous space radiation. This is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829760,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a solution that’s being considered for \ntrips to Mars, but would work well for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834080,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a non-relativistic interstellar trip. By the \nway, this is an upside of traveling relatively  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840440,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slowly—relatively minimal shielding is sufficient \nand bumping into a single dust grain doesn’t kill us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The last ingredient to add to our ship's biosphere \nis breathable air. Just as with water, recycling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854240,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is critical here. The ISS currently uses a system \ndesigned by the European Space Agency called the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859960,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Advanced Closed Loop System, which recycles \ncarbon dioxide back into breathable oxygen,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with around 50% efficiency. That’s \nnot nearly enough for a generation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869200,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ship because huge supplies of fresh oxygen would \nbe needed to replenish the losses. Instead,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875800,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’d probably need to rely heavily on our natural \nCO2 recyclers—the plants we are growing for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882120,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "food. There have been various efforts to build \nself-contained biospheres capable of sustaining  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887120,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a breathable atmosphere. Maybe the most famous is \nthe Biosphere 2 project, which did OK, all things  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893520,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "considered. Yes they had to install artificial \nCO2 scrubbers to help the plants, but the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "project at least demonstrated that a combination \nof natural and artificial systems could maintain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903640,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a breathable atmosphere for some time. We have \na few decades to perfect this, so there’s a good  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chance we can come up with an air recycling \nsystem that will work over long timescales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914760,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So maybe we can keep our crew alive \nand physically healthy for centuries,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or even millenia. But will they be \nhappy? And will they stay sane? The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925000,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sense of isolation on such a long voyage \nwill likely be a major challenge for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maintaining the mental health of the crew. \nWe need them to feel connected to Earth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935040,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be part of something grander than their \njanky little spacecraft on its lonely journey."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940360,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first generation in particular will want \nto stay connected to their loved ones. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the two-way light travel time between the ship \nand the Earth will increase over the journey,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949440,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ultimately reaching a lag of \nnearly 8.5 years near the end"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952980,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NASA has done some tests to mitigate \nthe dread that could follow from such separation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from our home world. One solution could be the \nuse of virtual reality. Crew members could find  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964960,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solace in digital 3D models of comforting and \nbeautiful Earth environments, and in the case  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of generation one, their homes and loved ones. \nAs the time lag increased, messages from friends  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and family and well-wishers could be recorded on \nEarth, beamed to the ship, and played back in VR."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982760,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On our cramped and sterile spaceship, it \nmay be important to grant our travelers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "certain experiences that we on Earth take \nfor granted. By improving the immersion and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interactivity of our VR technology we may be \nable to provide convincing visual experiences  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998400,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of mountains and sunsets, and auditory and \neven tactile experiences of wind and rain,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004680,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the olfactory joys of a forest or freshly \ncut grass. We can’t build a StarTrek holodeck,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but we can certainly push VR a lot \nfurther in the time we have before launch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course, the humans on the ship will \nstill be humans. Arguments will happen,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020440,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relationships will experience strain, and \nsensitivity and frustration levels may be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024760,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heightened due to the isolation and confined \nspaces. And yet a high level of synergy and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030080,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "teamwork is needed for this mission to succeed. \nSometimes a stressed human needs another human."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But maybe, when tensions rise and trust wanes, \nit would be helpful to have a trusted third  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040600,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "party to give advice, confide in, and to overall \nreceive encouragement from. One that remembers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049240,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and learns from the problems of \npast generations. Maybe we need  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053600,
    "dDurationMs": 9320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an AI therapist. NASA has already piloted such \na tool, namely Cimon 2.0 the therapy AI robot.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Preliminary testing seems promising and it \nis generally agreed upon that some tool or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1068600,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AI of this form will be incredibly important \nfor the success of a long term space mission."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1074320,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our plan so far will hopefully get our crew \nto Proxima-B in good health, genetically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1079160,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physically, and mentally. But how do we \nmake sure that the mission of the launch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083000,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crew is still the mission of the landing \ncrew? How do we ensure that the knowledge  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087120,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and skills needed to complete the mission \nare passed across generations? Or that we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093160,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "preserve the wealth of cultural knowledge \nand tradition of these once-Earthlings?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098720,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is where things get more speculative as there \nisn’t much research to go on. We just know that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104520,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this stuff is going to be very important \nand probably very tricky. The ship-bound  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109040,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "society is going to need a culture and social \nstructure that balances different needs.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114680,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That structure needs to enable efficient \noperation of the mission—which may mean  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1119040,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clear hierarchies in each operational area. \nBut the culture also needs to promote crew  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happiness—otherwise we have a revolution \nin a generation or two. So, an efficient  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and stable social structure that somehow also \npromotes mutual respect, individual freedoms,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1135240,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and all the various values that we want this \nnew branch of humanity to carry forward."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1139760,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Overall, it seems at least possible to build \na generation ship that can reach Proxima B,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1145240,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to launch in the not-to-distant future. There are \nso many things that we know could go wrong—and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1150880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no doubt many more unknown fail points. And the \nlonger the mission, the more risk of unexpected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1157280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disaster, so maybe we should really focus on \ngetting fusion on track. But it’s encouraging  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1162960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to think that this sort of sci-fi endeavor \nis at least within our grasp if existential  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1168960,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need or our adventurous spirit compels \nus. We are the middle children of history,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1174760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but perhaps we’re ready to grow up. Perhaps \nsoon our generation ships will slip the bonds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1180360,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gravity and distance to explore the \nnew frontier of interstellar spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1186880,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. If you enjoyed today’s episode \nthen I highly recommend you check out the new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1190560,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feature length documentary “Space: The Longest \nGoodbye.” The film explores the realities that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1196440,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NASA’s goal to send astronauts to Mars would \nrequire a three-year absence from Earth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1202160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which would be twice as long as the current record \nfor consecutive time in space. Bridging the gap  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1207520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between the astronauts who dream of space \ntravel and the psychologists whose job is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1212320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to keep astronauts mentally stable in outer \nspace, the documentary vividly displays how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1217840,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those who dream of space travel must balance \ntheir dream of reaching new frontiers and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1223560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "harsh psychological realities of space. \nThe film is now available on YouTube so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1228840,
    "dDurationMs": 7673,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "check out the link below. After June 4th, it \nwill be available exclusively on the PBS app."
    } ]
  } ]
}
